  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Several rounds of showers and thunderstorms return \nTuesday into Wednesday with an increasing potential for severe \nweather and flash flooding.\n\nBeginning early Tuesday morning, a warm front will lifts northward \nthroughout the day. As this warm front lifts northward, winds turn \nmore southerly with moisture being transported northward. PWAT \nvalues are forecast to surge upwards of 2.00-2.25 behind the front. \nAs this front lifts north, hi res guidance continues to show \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms to develop along the \nfront, bringing periods of heavy downpours during the morning rush \nhour. Areas of greatest concern will be mainly around the I-78 and I-\n80 corridors across northeast PA and northern NJ, some areas of \nwhich were hit hard with flash flooding just this past weekend. At \nsome point, the warm front will stall nearby the NJ/NY/PA border, \nbut it is uncertain where. The area of greatest flash flood risk \nwill likely be where the front stalls. A general 1-2 inches of rain \ncan be expected from this first of many rounds of precipitation with \nlocally higher amounts. While this may not sound significant, many \nlocations, especially in northern NJ, remain vulnerable to excessive \nrainfall after this past weekend. As such, rainfall rates of 1-2 \ninches per hour at times will be sufficient to result in scattered \nincidents of flash flooding during the critical morning rush hour \nTuesday. Furthermore, there is a nonzero severe threat with these \nstorms given the deep layer shear present along with the presence of \nthe frontal boundary. All this said, high uncertainty remains with \nthe timing and overall coverage of the morning convection. \n\nAs the day progresses into the late morning and the afternoon, some \nguidance continues to develop additional isolated showers and \nthunderstorms across the area. The second round of thunderstorms \nwill be developing across PA during the afternoon hours, which carry \nthe greatest severe potential with widespread coverage as an upper \nlevel trough moves through. With PWAT values near or over 2 inches \nalong with instability building to around 2000-3000 J/kg or greater \nand deep layer shear in the 30-40 kt range, the environment will be \nfavorable for severe thunderstorms with the primary hazard being \ndamaging wind gusts, though a tornado or two cannot be ruled out. \nWith that potential, the Storm Prediction Center has upgraded the \nregion to an an ENHANCED (3 out of 5) risk for the entire region. \nThe fly in the ointment for the severe potential will be how robust \nthe morning convection ends up being. More thunderstorm coverage in \nthe morning could mean less severe potential for the I-78 corridor \nand north should the atmosphere not recover in time. Regardless, the \nrisk for severe weather remains with uncertainty remaining elevated \nas well. What is more certain, however, is the potential for \ntorrential downpours from these thunderstorms. Rainfall rates around \n2 inches per hour will be possible with this round of storms as \nwell, bringing the risk of flash flooding to the rest of the region. \n\nAs this second round of storms move through the region, hi res \nguidance continues to suggest additional rounds of storms thereafter \nas the cold front pushes in from the west, in which a disorganized \nand chaotic storm mode could begin to take hold by the evening into \novernight hours. The severe risk will begin to gradually diminish \nthrough the evening as the environment gets worked over, but \nflooding concerns will persist through at least 2AM with the \ncontinue risk for heavy downpours and the possibility of training \nstorms given the messy and widespread coverage of thunderstorms \nexpected over the urban corridor. Thunderstorm coverage could \ncontinue into the early morning hours of Wednesday, but hi res \nguidance suggests most storms will be concentrated along the coastal \nplains by that point. By the time we reach the overnight hours, an \nadditional 1-2 inches could have fallen across the region with \nlocally higher amounts.\n\nGiven the potential for flash flooding across the urban corridor, a \nFlash Flood Watch was issued from 6AM Tuesday through 2AM Wednesday \nmorning.\n\nOn Wednesday, the SPC and WPC continue to have portions of \nDelmarva and southern NJ outlooked for severe weather and a risk\nfor excessive rainfall, respectively. Part of this set-up will \ndepend on the placement of the front and timing but latest CAM\nguidance continues to emphasize re-development of convection\nalong the coastal plains of Delmarva and New Jersey through much\nof the day Wednesday. Primary threat with any severe storms\nis likely to be damaging wind gusts in addition to heavy \nrainfall leading to flash flooding, particularly in the more \nurbanized areas along the immediate coast. \n\nOnce the front moves away by late Wednesday, we are drier for \nThursday and beyond into the weekend.",
